    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_7f17a8c9ee5934e363f8ca8792554664f8f4152541ec64c0bcac9f59836edd74",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Heath-Brown's fractional-moment argument and the later prime-polynomial arguments share a surrogate-plus-controlled-failure architecture: each replaces a difficult zeta expression by finite Dirichlet data, then pays separately for what the replacement does not control, through smoothed convexity bounds on an explicit discrepancy in the first method and negligible exceptional sets in the multiscale method.",
